== 8.7 Chapter 8 Practice Drill — 30 Questions

30 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 33 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [A comparative answer differs from two descriptions because it],
  [is longer],
  [tests one claim on both texts and reaches a verdict],
  [names both authors],
  [uses more quotations]
)

#mcq(2, [The four columns of the shared grid are],
  [author, date, title, genre],
  [plot, character, theme, style],
  [introduction, body, conclusion, summary],
  [claim, text A, text B, verdict]
)

#mcq(3, [A verdict that survives the removal of the two middle columns is],
  [required],
  [typical],
  [suspect, because the comparison was doing no work],
  [strong]
)

#mcq(4, [“Compare the treatment of X in A and B” wants],
  [a summary of both plots],
  [one claim, two mechanisms, a verdict],
  [a preference],
  [two descriptions]
)

#mcq(5, [“Which text is more effective” first requires],
  [a summary],
  [a quotation from each text],
  [a preference],
  [a criterion for effectiveness]
)

#mcq(6, [“How far do A and B agree that …” wants],
  [agreement only],
  [a summary of both plots],
  [agreement and disagreement in proportion],
  [a verdict without evidence]
)

#mcq(7, [The difference sentence has the form],
  [“A and B are similar”],
  [“both … but where A …, B …”],
  [“A is about … and B is about …”],
  [“A is better than B”]
)

#mcq(8, [If you have read only one of the two texts named, the best policy is],
  [to answer fully on the text you know and compare at the level of function],
  [to answer on neither],
  [to invent examples from the other],
  [to write about the author instead]
)

#mcq(9, [A fabricated quotation is],
  [the one error a marker cannot forgive],
  [better than no quotation],
  [acceptable if it sounds plausible],
  [common in high-scoring scripts]
)

#mcq(10, [Describing a moment instead of quoting it is],
  [acceptable, and much better than inventing a line],
  [always wrong],
  [a sign of laziness],
  [preferred by markers]
)

#mcq(11, [Forty questions in forty minutes allows about],
  [90 seconds per question],
  [30 seconds per question],
  [54 seconds per question],
  [2 minutes per question]
)

#mcq(12, [The recommended first two minutes of the paper are spent],
  [reading the paper and marking the easiest set-text questions],
  [checking the answer sheet],
  [answering question one],
  [writing a plan]
)

#mcq(13, [The recommended order of work is],
  [hardest first],
  [judgement questions first],
  [unseen passages and fact questions, then function, effect and judgement],
  [in printed order strictly]
)

#mcq(14, [A blank answer costs],
  [a certain mark],
  [half a mark],
  [a mark only if the paper is negative-marked],
  [nothing]
)

#mcq(15, [In the last minute, the worst action is],
  [changing every answer in panic],
  [guessing an answer],
  [leaving a blank],
  [checking the numbering]
)

#mcq(16, [The longest option on a multiple-choice question is],
  [always correct],
  [always wrong],
  [often a near-miss with a qualifying clause],
  [never worth reading]
)

#mcq(17, [Reading the stem twice and the options once is a mistake because],
  [reading is slow],
  [the options are always wrong],
  [the stem is unimportant],
  [the options carry the trap]
)

#mcq(18, [A comparison between a play and a novel must allow for],
  [the different lengths],
  [the different kinds of evidence available in each form],
  [the authors' nationalities],
  [the publication dates]
)

#mcq(19, [“Both texts present grief” is not yet a comparison because],
  [it uses abstract nouns],
  [it names no character],
  [it is false],
  [it states a shared ground without a difference or a verdict]
)

#mcq(20, [A criterion in a “more effective” answer must be],
  [the author's own view],
  [defensible and applied consistently to both texts],
  [quoted from the text],
  [popular with markers]
)

#mcq(21, [The best time to decide which text is “more effective” is],
  [after applying the criterion to both texts],
  [never — the question cannot be answered],
  [in the conclusion only],
  [before defining the criterion]
)

#mcq(22, [Comparing two texts that share a theme but differ in genre requires attention to],
  [the settings only],
  [the number of pages],
  [the authors' biographies],
  [how each form makes its claim available to the audience]
)

#mcq(23, [A comparative essay's paragraphs are usually organised by],
  [text, one after the other],
  [point of comparison, with both texts in each paragraph],
  [chapter order],
  [length]
)

#mcq(24, [Conceding a point to the text you are arguing against],
  [should be avoided in short answers],
  [strengthens it, because it shows the criterion has been applied honestly],
  [is unnecessary],
  [weakens the answer]
)

#mcq(25, [The “one-text protocol” recommends adding the missing text],
  [only as a single functional contrast, kept short],
  [as a quotation],
  [as a summary],
  [as a biography]
)

#mcq(26, [The highest-value use of the last two minutes is],
  [filling blanks and checking that the answer sheet matches the numbering],
  [writing a conclusion],
  [rereading the passage],
  [changing answers]
)

#mcq(27, [A candidate who spends eleven minutes on the first five questions has],
  [improved accuracy],
  [banked the cheapest marks],
  [used the recommended plan],
  [lost about nine minutes that later questions needed]
)

#mcq(28, [The exam-hall rule about quotations is],
  [use as many as possible],
  [six to ten exact words, embedded, one per point],
  [quote only from the last scene],
  [quote whole speeches]
)

#mcq(29, [A judgement question answered in ten seconds is],
  [always correct],
  [a guess with extra words],
  [efficient],
  [the recommended method]
)

#mcq(30, [The best preparation for the exam hall is],
  [memorising summaries],
  [learning quotations by heart],
  [practising the full paper under time and reviewing the key],
  [reading the texts again]
)

#shortcut-box("Before you turn to the key")[
  + Mark the whole set in *one pass* — every answer, right or wrong — before you read a single solution.
  + Write the score at the top of the page, because a drill you cannot compare with the last one tells you nothing.
  + Then read *every* solution, including the ones you got right: the second method is in the solution, not in the chapter.
]

